{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "ER Model to Relational Model in DBMS"}, {"type": "position", "content": 11}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 2}, {"type": "level", "content": "B1.3"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you hear/use when talking about ER Model to Relational Model in DBMS."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Exchange written and spoken information on principles, concepts, data base objects related to relational database management system in spoken or written texts.   Describe relational database, its concepts, and principles in spoken or written texts. "}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Discusses information a wider range of contexts about exchanging technical information on software programming and software solutions, using appropriate grammar and vocabulary at this level.  Recognizes simple ideas on concepts on software programming, software solutions and database management."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe7n7le02ob11re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Passive of Modal Verbs with Have to"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "The <b>passive voice</b> is a form of speech in which you focus on the action itself rather than on the person who performs such action. <b>Passive voice</b> with modals is used to change the intention of the sentence: <b>have to</b> is a <b>modal verb</b> we use to express obligation, something that <b>has to be done</b>."}, {"type": "text", "content": "When we use the <b>passive voice</b> with <strong> have to</strong>,  our main goal is to say that there is an <strong> obligation</strong>  to do something we <strong> need</strong>  to do. </br></br>  To use the <b>passive voice</b> with<strong> have to</strong>  you need to follow this structure: </br></br>  Object + have/has to + verb be + main verb past participle + complement + subject (if known or necessary) </br></br>  Example: </br> <i>These data <strong> have to be included</strong> in the report.</i> </br></br>  We use <b>don't have to</b>  to say that it is not necessary to do something, it is not an obligation. To use <strong> don't have to</strong>   follow this structure: </br></br>  Object + doesn't/don't have to + verb be + main verb past participle + complement + subject (if known or necessary) </br></br>  Example: </br> <i>These data <strong> don't have to</strong>  be modified by today, it's okay if you do it tomorrow.</i> </br></br>  To form questions using <b>passive voice</b> and the modal verb <strong> have to</strong>, we use the auxiliary verb <strong> do/does</strong>  in the present simple: </br>  Do/does + object + have to + verb be + main verb past participle + complement + subject (if known or necessary) </br></br>  Example: </br> <I><b>Does</b> this software for compiling data <b>have to be</b> fixed?</i>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Redundancy and irregularities have to be removed from the model you developed by making sure that the same data is not stored in multiple parts of the diagram."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Entities in the ER diagram have to be converted into tables. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Each attribute has to be converted into a column of the table."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Questions within Questions"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "Questions within questions, or <b>indirect questions</b> are commonly used in polite situations or when talking to a person we don't know well. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "These type of questions have two parts. The first part is usually an <b>introductory phrase</b>. The second part is the <b>indirect question</b>.</br></br>  If you want to use <b>indirect questions</b>, there are some rules you need to remember: </br></br> To ask an <b>indirect question</b> using the verb <strong>is</strong>  or <strong>are</strong>  the verb comes after the subject. Not before the subject like in regular or normal questions. </br></br>  Example: </br></br>  <strong><i>Regular question</strong> : </br> Where <strong>are</strong>  the data? </br>  <strong>Indirect question</strong> : </br> Do you know where the data <strong>are</strong> ?</i> </br></br>  In <b>indirect questions</b> we don't use auxiliary verbs like <strong>do, did</strong>  or<strong> does</strong> ; also the main verb is conjugated as if you were using it in an affirmative sentence. </br></br>  Example: </br></br>  <strong><i>Regular question</strong> : </br> What <strong>does</strong> he have to do to turn this ER model into a relational model? </br></br>  <strong>Indirect question</strong> : </br> Can you tell him what he <strong>has</strong>  to do to turn this ER model into a relational model?</i>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Could you tell me how I can turn an ER hierarchical entity set into a table in the relational model?"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Do you know if ER diagrams can be mapped into relational schema?"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "get the hang of something "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to learn how to do something "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(idiom)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I think I'm getting the hang of it now, thanks!"}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n7le02ob11aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "tons "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a large extent, amount, or quantity of something"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I have tons of information about that."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n7le02ob11aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "by all means "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "it has a similar meaning to that of absolutely"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(idiom)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: By all means! I have tons of information about that."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n7le02ob11aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "for sure "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "synonym for certainly or unquestionably"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(idiom)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: For sure you can! There are automated and manual ways to do it."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n7le02ob11aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "independent "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "not connected with another or with each other; separate"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Entities that are represented by the rectangular box have to be changed to independent tables."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n7le02ob11aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "pretty "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "synonym for quite or very"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adverb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: That one is pretty easy!"}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n7le02ob11aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Look for more information about ER Diagram and relational table. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Create a post with the main characteristics of ER Diagram and relational table. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Share with your partners the post you created."}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I look for more information about ER Diagram and relational table. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I create a post with the main characteristics of ER Diagram and relational table. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I share with your partners the post I created."}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "type": "welcome", "_data": [], "contents": [{}], "id": 0}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"time": "0", "image": "enpe7n7le02ob11re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<div align=\"justify\"><strong>Macey</strong>: Hey Sophie! What's up? </br>\n<strong>Sophie</strong>: I'm worried about the test. Can you tell me how I can turn from the ER model to the relational model? </br>\n<strong>Macey</strong>: By all means! I have tons of information about that. Look, turning from the ER model to the relational model has to be done following two major steps. First, redundancy and irregularities have to be removed from the model you developed by making sure that the same data is not stored in multiple parts of the diagram. And second, the model has to be simplified to make it compatible to the relational model, so we need to convert or even remove certain attributes to fit in the relational model. </br></div>"}, {"time": "47000", "image": "enpe7n7le02ob11re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<div align=\"justify\"><strong>Sophie</strong>: Do you know if ER diagrams can be mapped into relational schema? </br>\n<strong>Macey</strong>: For sure they can! There are automated and manual ways to do it. First, entities in the ER diagram have to be converted into tables. So all entities that are represented by the rectangular box have to be changed to independent tables. Then, each attribute has to be converted into a column of the table. </br>\n<strong>Sophie</strong>: Cool! I have a specific question, could you tell me how I can turn an ER hierarchical entity set into a table in the relational model? </br></div>"}, {"time": "87500", "image": "enpe7n7le02ob11re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<div align=\"justify\"><strong>Macey</strong>: That one is pretty easy! First, you create a table for higher-level and lower-level entities. Then, primary keys of higher-level entities have to be added in the table of lower-level entities. After that, add all other attributes of lower-level entities in lower-level tables. Next, you declare primary key of higher-level table and the primary key for lower-level table. And finally, you declare foreign key constraints. </br>\n<strong>Sophie</strong>: I think I'm getting the hang of it now, thanks! </br>\n<strong>Macey</strong>: You're welcome!</div>"}], "coverimg": "enpe7n7le02ob11re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n7le02ob11re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 1, "title": ""}], "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "questions": [], "type": "WLC", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can talk about turning from ER model to relational model.  "}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can discuss how to turn diagrams from the ER model into tables in the relational model.   "}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can talk about how to turn an ER hierarchical entity set into a table in the relational model"}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "In your opinion, what is the best way to organize data? "}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "Would you rather have data displayed on a map or on a table?"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "group_name", "content": "Welcome"}], "type": "group"}, {"type": "feedback", "content": {"attempt_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "You have 2 tries"}, {"type": "text", "content": "you have 1 attempt left"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct", "incorrect_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "try again"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Maybe next time"}], "allowed_attempts": 2}}, {"type": "free_header_text", "content": "Ask your partner:"}, {"type": "free_type_text_area", "content": "Changing From One Model To Another"}], "im